**Goal** — Add the pure `isAllowedThreadDocPath` allowlist, the protocol types for the thread write path, and make `parseAssign` accept `PUBLISH` / `DISCARD` actions and an optional `title`.

**Motivation** — Phase B threads are read-only; the protocol and ASSIGN parser must express writing, publishing and discarding before any runner behaviour can be built, and the deployed API must keep working with a C1 runner.

**Approach** — In `packages/fleet-protocol` (new file `src/thread-doc-paths.ts`, exported from `src/index.ts`):

```ts
export const THREAD_DOC_LIMITS = { maxFileBytes: 524_288, maxPathChars: 512 } as const;
/** Pure, no I/O. `feature` is the thread's feature slug. */
export function isAllowedThreadDocPath(path: string, feature: string): boolean;
```

`isAllowedThreadDocPath(path, feature)` is `true` exactly when all hold:

1. `path` is a string of 1..`maxPathChars` characters with no `\`, no NUL and no leading `/`;
2. every `/`-separated segment matches `/^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$/` and is not `.` or `..`, and no segment equals `.git` (any letter case);
3. the last segment ends in `.md` and is longer than `.md`;
4. either the first segment is `docs` and there are at least 2 segments, or the path starts with `.nax/features/<feature>/` and there are at least 4 segments.

Mirror the pattern of `packages/fleet-protocol/src/nax-config-paths.ts` (`naxPathGroup` / `isAllowedNaxPath`).

In `src/threads.ts`:

```ts
export type ThreadAction = 'SESSION' | 'PUBLISH' | 'DISCARD';
export type ThreadPublishFailReason = 'branch_moved' | 'repo_busy' | 'no_worktree' | 'git_error';
export type ThreadDiscardFailReason = 'no_worktree' | 'git_error';
// ThreadAssign gains: title?: string;
export type ThreadRunnerLine =
  | { type: 'session_started'; at: string; resumed: boolean; backend: ThreadBackend; skillShas: string[]; stale?: true }
  | { type: 'session_closed'; at: string; reason: ThreadCloseReason }
  | { type: 'input_consumed'; at: string; messageId: string }
  | { type: 'session_failed'; at: string; reason: ThreadFailReason }
  | { type: 'write_doc'; at: string; path: string; bytes: number }
  | { type: 'published'; at: string; sha: string; paths: string[]; ignored: string[]; specPresent: boolean }
  | { type: 'publish_failed'; at: string; reason: ThreadPublishFailReason }
  | { type: 'discarded'; at: string; tipSha: string }
  | { type: 'discard_failed'; at: string; reason: ThreadDiscardFailReason };
```

`RunnerCapabilities` in `src/index.ts` gains `threadWrite?: true`. The `THREAD_PUBLISH` payload is `{}`; the runner ignores its content.

ASSIGN parsing: `parseAssign` (`apps/runner/src/supervisor/assign-parser.ts`) accepts `thread.action` `SESSION`, `PUBLISH` or `DISCARD`; any other value is `invalid thread action`. For `PUBLISH` and `DISCARD`, a non-null `initialMessage` is `invalid initialMessage`. `thread.title`, when present, must be a string of 1..200 characters (else `invalid thread title`) and is copied; when absent the parsed `ThreadAssign` has no `title` key. All other `thread` fields keep the B3b rules for every action.

Update the existing test at `apps/runner/src/supervisor/assign-parser-thread.spec.ts:68` (asserted `PUBLISH` -> `invalid thread action`) into a test that `thread.action: 'PUBLISH'` with `initialMessage: null` returns an `assign.thread` whose `action` is `'PUBLISH'`; an unknown action such as `'REVIEW'` still returns `invalid thread action`.

**Scope** — In: allowlist, protocol types, `RunnerCapabilities.threadWrite` type, `parseAssign` changes. Out: event-writer methods (US-002), the `write_doc` tool (US-003), Publish (US-004), worktree/Discard (US-005), the capability probe reporting `threadWrite: true` (US-007).

**Interface** — see Approach (verbatim).

**Goal** — Add `publishThreadDocs`, which commits exactly the allowlisted files a thread changed onto `refs/heads/feat/<feature>` in the runner's clone without pushing.

**Motivation** — The runner owns the checkout, git identity and repo mutex, so the commit that turns a brainstorm into `.nax/features/<feature>/spec.md` on the local feature branch belongs here.

**Approach** — Rulings: C1-R4 Publish never waits on the repo mutex (if another job holds it, fail at once with `repo_busy`). C1-D1 Publish keeps `refs/heads/feat/<feature>` and the worktree's `HEAD` together: it compares the branch with the worktree `HEAD` at publish time: an absent branch is created, a branch equal to `HEAD` is advanced, any other value is `branch_moved`. No session-start state is persisted. C1-D3 the commit title is the thread title.

`publishThreadDocs(input)` in new `apps/runner/src/thread/thread-publish.ts`:

```ts
export interface PublishThreadDocsInput {
  readonly git: Git;
  readonly mutex: Pick<RepoMutex, 'acquire' | 'isLocked'>;
  readonly repoKey: string;
  readonly worktree: string;
  readonly feature: string;
  readonly threadId: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly identity: GitIdentity;
}
export type PublishThreadDocsResult =
  | { ok: true; sha: string; paths: string[]; ignored: string[]; specPresent: boolean }
  | { ok: false; reason: ThreadPublishFailReason; detail?: string };
export async function publishThreadDocs(input: PublishThreadDocsInput): Promise<PublishThreadDocsResult>;
```

Steps, all git calls with `cwd: input.worktree`:

1. `<worktree>/.git` must exist and `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` in `worktree` must equal `realpath(worktree)`, else `no_worktree` (a plain directory inside some other repository is never treated as the worktree).
2. If `mutex.isLocked(repoKey)`: `repo_busy`, and `acquire` is not called. Otherwise acquire it (granted at once) and release it in a `finally`, so every result and every throw after this step leaves the key unlocked.
3. Dirty set: entries of `git status --porcelain -z --untracked-files=all`, each path taken after its two-character status and space (a rename's second NUL field is skipped). `paths` = entries that pass `isAllowedThreadDocPath(entry, feature)` and exist as regular files (`lstat`, not a symlink); `ignored` = every other entry, including a deleted file. Both sorted.
4. `head` = `git rev-parse HEAD`; `current` = the sha of `refs/heads/feat/<feature>`, or `null` when `git show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/feat/<feature>` exits 1 (any other non-zero exit is `git_error`). `current !== null && current !== head` -> `branch_moved`.
5. `paths` empty: when `current === null`, `git update-ref refs/heads/feat/<feature> <head> <zero sha>` (the zero sha is 40 `0` characters: the ref must not exist yet); result `sha` = `head`.
6. `paths` not empty: `git add -- <paths>`, then `git commit ... -- <paths>` (only `paths`, even if something else is staged) with the explicit identity (as `commitAndPushConfig` in `executor/config-job/commit-push.ts`: `-c user.name=… -c user.email=… -c commit.gpgsign=false commit --no-verify` plus the `GIT_AUTHOR_*` / `GIT_COMMITTER_*` env), message `docs(<feature>): spec from koda thread "<title>"`, a blank line, and the trailer `Koda-Thread: <threadId>`. `title` has CR / LF replaced by spaces and is cut to 200 characters. `newSha` = `git rev-parse HEAD`; then `git update-ref refs/heads/feat/<feature> <newSha> <current ?? zero sha>`; a non-zero exit is `branch_moved`, after `git reset --soft <head>` moves the worktree back so its HEAD again matches what the branch was compared with.
7. `specPresent` = `git cat-file -e <sha>:.nax/features/<feature>/spec.md` exits 0.
8. A git call that fails outside the rules above is `git_error` with `detail` passed through `sanitizeDiagnostic`. Every git call has `timeoutMs` `PUBLISH_GIT_TIMEOUT_MS` (60 000, a constant in `thread-publish.ts`). No step pushes, fetches or touches a remote.

**Scope** — In: `thread-publish.ts` and its tests on real temporary git repos. Out: calling it from a live session (US-006) or a PUBLISH job (US-007); Discard (US-005). Also out for the whole feature: Publish never waits for the repo mutex, never pushes or fetches.

**Interface** — see Approach (verbatim).

**Goal** — Make a session start 'stale' on the worktree's old commit instead of failing when the checkout would overwrite local changes, and add `discardThreadChanges` that resets the thread's unpublished files and re-detaches the worktree at the branch tip.

**Motivation** — Today a PLAN or RUN that moves the feature branch under unpublished thread files makes session start fail with `worktree_conflict`; the user needs to keep their files and later discard them.

**Approach** — `prepareThreadWorktree` (`apps/runner/src/thread/thread-worktree.ts`): when the existing worktree's `git checkout --detach <tipSha>` exits non-zero with stderr saying local changes or untracked files `would be overwritten` (`createGit` forces `LC_ALL=C`, so the message is stable; the same file already matches `REF_LOCK_ERROR` this way), the result is `{ ok: true, worktree, tipSha: <the worktree's HEAD sha>, stale: true }`, the worktree is left as it was, and the returned `tipSha` is what the session starts on. Any other non-zero checkout keeps `{ ok: false, reason: 'worktree_conflict' }`. A checkout that succeeds, and a freshly added worktree, give `stale: false`. Every other failure keeps its B3a reason.

Types: `ThreadWorktreeResult` becomes `{ ok: true; worktree; tipSha; stale: boolean }` (plus the existing failure arm). `ThreadPrepareResult` (`thread/thread-job-run.ts:40`) becomes `{ ok: true; worktree; tipSha; stale?: boolean }`, absent meaning `false`; optional so the existing `prepareWorktree` test stubs still type-check.

`discardThreadChanges(input)` in new `apps/runner/src/thread/thread-discard.ts`:

```ts
export interface DiscardThreadChangesInput {
  readonly git: Git;
  readonly homeDir: string;
  readonly repoDir: string;
  readonly threadId: string;
  readonly feature: string;
  readonly baseRef: string;
}
export type DiscardThreadChangesResult =
  | { ok: true; tipSha: string }
  | { ok: false; reason: ThreadDiscardFailReason; detail?: string };
export async function discardThreadChanges(input: DiscardThreadChangesInput): Promise<DiscardThreadChangesResult>;
```

Steps, in `threadWorktreeDir(homeDir, threadId)`: `<wt>/.git` must exist and `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` there must equal `realpath(<wt>)`, else `no_worktree` (as Publish step 1); `git reset --hard HEAD`; `git clean -fd` (no `-x`, so ignored files survive); resolve the tip with `resolveTip`, now exported from `thread-worktree.ts`, in the order `prepareThreadWorktree` uses (local `refs/heads/feat/<feature>`, then `refs/remotes/origin/<baseRef>`, then `<baseRef>^{commit}`, all read in `repoDir`); `git checkout --detach <tip>`; return `tipSha`. No fetch and no repo mutex: only the thread's own worktree changes. A git failure is `git_error` with a sanitized `detail`.

Existing test to update: `apps/runner/test/unit/thread-worktree.spec.ts:174` (expected `{ ok: false, reason: 'worktree_conflict' }` when the existing worktree's checkout is refused) becomes a test expecting a result matching `{ ok: true, stale: true }` with `tipSha` equal to the worktree's HEAD; a refused `worktree add` still fails.

**Scope** — In: `thread-worktree.ts` changes, `thread-discard.ts`, the `ThreadPrepareResult` type. Out: no rebase of base-ref drift (a stale session stays on its old HEAD until Discard); announcing `stale` on `session_started` (US-006); the DISCARD job path and `host.discard` wiring (US-007).

**Interface** — see Approach (verbatim).
